          Searches for "treadmill for dogs" come from two kinds of owner: one with a high-energy dog
          and a long winter, wondering whether the treadmill in the spare room can help, and one
          who has seen a dog trotting on a treadmill online and wants to know whether it is safe.
          The honest equipment answer is that a human treadmill can be used by some dogs, under
          close supervision, with real compromises and a short list of rules that cannot be bent —
          and that for many dogs, and many owners, a purpose-built dog treadmill or simply more
          walks is the better answer.
        </p>

        <div id="short-answer" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">1. Can a Dog Use a Human Treadmill? The Short Answer</h2>
<p>
            <strong>Some dogs can, with supervision and compromises; it is never as good as a
            machine built for the job, and it is wrong for some dogs altogether.</strong> A human
            treadmill has a belt that is short for a running dog's stride, a motor hood at the
            front that a nose can hit, a gap at the rear roller where a paw or tail can be drawn
            in, a minimum speed that may be too fast for a small dog, and no side panels to keep
            the dog centred. It also has a safety key designed to be pulled by a falling human,
            which a dog cannot use.
          </p>
          <p>
            None of that makes it impossible. Medium and larger dogs that are healthy, adult,
            calm and gradually introduced can walk on a home treadmill at a slow speed with an
            owner standing beside them, and many owners use one that way to supplement walks in bad
            weather or during a period of restricted outdoor exercise. The rules that make that
            acceptable are not negotiable: the dog is never tethered to the machine or to anything
            else, never left for a moment, always able to step off, always started at the lowest
            speed, and always stopped at the first sign of stress. The full list is below.
          </p>
          <p>
            Before any of it, a vet should confirm the dog is a suitable candidate. Puppies with
            growing joints, senior dogs, flat-faced breeds and any dog with a heart, joint or
            breathing condition are plainly the wrong candidates for a moving belt, and the
            equipment compromises make a human treadmill a worse choice for small dogs in
            particular. If the goal is daily running rather than an occasional supplementary
            walk, a purpose-built dog treadmill is the right tool, and the section near the end of this page on when to
            buy one explains why.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="how-dog-treadmills-differ" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">2. How Dog-Specific Treadmills Differ From Human Ones</h2>
<p>
            Understanding what a dog treadmill changes is the clearest way to see what a human
            treadmill gets wrong for a dog. The differences are not cosmetic; each one addresses a
            specific problem with putting a four-legged animal on a belt designed for a two-legged
            one.
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>A longer deck, sized to a dog's stride.</strong> A trotting or running dog
            covers far more belt per stride than a walking human, with all four feet landing in
            sequence. Dog treadmills, particularly those built for larger breeds, have running
            surfaces that are typically longer than a home human treadmill, and they are sized by
            the dog's length rather than by a human's step.</li>
            <li><strong>A lower deck.</strong> Many dog treadmills sit closer to the floor, so the
            dog steps on and off at a natural height and a stumble is a short drop, not a fall from
            a raised platform.</li>
            <li><strong>Side rails or panels.</strong> Most have low walls or rails along both
            sides of the belt. These keep the dog centred, give it a visual boundary, stop it
            stepping sideways off a moving belt, and provide the lateral guidance a human gets from
            their own awareness of the deck edges.</li>
            <li><strong>Quieter drive.</strong> Motor whine, belt rumble and the click of a speed
            change are startling to many dogs. Dog treadmills are typically designed to run more
            quietly, and the quiet matters more than it would for a person wearing headphones.</li>
            <li><strong>Slower minimum speeds.</strong> Many dog treadmills start well below the
            typical human minimum, so a small or nervous dog can begin at a genuine amble.</li>
            <li><strong>No front motor hood at nose height.</strong> On a human treadmill the motor
            cover rises at the front of the belt, directly where a dog's head is. Dog treadmills
            are laid out so there is nothing for a nose to hit when the dog drifts forward.</li>
            <li><strong>Often manual or slatted designs.</strong> A good proportion of dog
            treadmills, especially for working and sporting dogs, are non-motorised: the dog's own
            movement drives the belt, so the belt only moves as fast as the dog does and stops when
            the dog stops. That removes the central hazard of a motorised belt — a belt that keeps
            going when the dog does not. The trade-off is that the dog must learn to drive it. Our
            guide to
            <a href="/manual-vs-motorized-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">manual versus motorised treadmills</a>
            explains the mechanical difference, which applies equally to the dog versions.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            Every one of those features exists because someone found out what happens without it.
            A human treadmill has none of them, which is why the rest of this page is about the
            compromises and the rules.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="why-human-treadmill-compromise" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">3. Why a Human Treadmill Is a Compromise for a Dog</h2>
<p>
            Taking the differences above in turn, here is what a home treadmill actually presents
            to a dog, feature by feature.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>The belt is short for a running dog.</strong> A human treadmill belt is sized so
            a person can walk or run with a little room ahead and behind. A dog of any size trots
            with a longer effective stride than a walking human, and at a run it needs more belt
            than most home decks provide. The practical consequence is that a human treadmill
            suits a dog for <em>walking</em> and perhaps a gentle trot, and not for running. Belt
            dimensions are the thing to check first, and our
            <a href="/treadmill-belt-size-guide/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmill belt size guide</a>
            explains what the length and width figures mean. For a large dog, even a generously
            sized human deck is marginal at anything above a walk.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>The rear roller gap and the side strips.</strong> At the back of the deck the
            belt wraps under a roller, and there is a narrow gap where the belt disappears. A
            trailing paw, a long tail, a dew claw or a tuft of fur can be drawn towards that gap
            if the dog drifts backwards. Along the sides, the fixed foot rails sit beside the
            moving belt, and a paw that lands half on the rail and half on the belt is twisted.
            Humans avoid both by instinct; a dog has to be kept centred by the owner, because the
            machine offers no side guidance.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>The motor hood at the front.</strong> A dog that is anxious or trying to reach
            the owner tends to move forward on the belt, and on a human treadmill the forward limit
            is a hard plastic cover at head height. A nose or chest bumping the hood is unpleasant
            and startling, and a startled dog on a moving belt is the beginning of most accidents.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Minimum speed may be too fast.</strong> Human treadmills typically start
            somewhere around half a mile an hour to one mile an hour. For a large dog that is a
            slow amble; for a small dog it can already be a trot, with no slower option. Our guide
            to
            <a href="/how-fast-do-treadmills-go/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">how fast treadmills go</a>
            covers the typical speed range of home machines, and the bottom of that range is the
            figure that matters here.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Noise and startle.</strong> The belt starts with a lurch on many machines, the
            motor note changes with speed, and the cooling fan and roller noise are constant. A
            dog's hearing makes all of this louder than it is to you, and a sudden sound at the
            wrong moment can make a dog jump sideways off a moving belt.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>No side guidance and no boundary.</strong> Without rails the dog has nothing to
            tell it where the belt ends, and no physical cue to stay in the middle. The owner is
            the side rail, which is why supervision from beside the machine, not from in front of
            it, is in the rules.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>The safety key is for a human.</strong> The tethered key stops the belt when a
            falling person pulls it. A dog cannot be clipped to it, and should never be clipped to
            anything on the machine, so the emergency stop for a dog is the owner's hand on the
            key or the stop button. If the owner is not standing right there, there is no
            emergency stop at all. Our guide to the
            <a href="/treadmill-safety-key-not-working/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmill safety key</a>
            explains how the interlock works; for a dog session, the key should be in and the
            owner's hand should be on it.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="safety-rules" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">4. The Non-Negotiable Safety Rules</h2>
<p>
            If, after a vet has agreed the dog is a suitable candidate, you decide to use a human
            treadmill with it, these rules are the price. They are not a list to pick from.
          </p>
          <ol>
            <li><strong>Never tether the dog to the machine.</strong> No leash, lead, line or collar
            attachment to the console, the handrails, the frame or the safety key. A tethered dog
            that stumbles is dragged, and a leash caught in a roller is worse. The same applies to
            tethering the dog to anything near the machine — a wall hook, a piece of furniture, a
            person's wrist. The dog must be free.</li>
            <li><strong>Never leave the dog unattended, not for a moment.</strong> Not to answer the
            door, not to fetch a treat, not to check a phone. The owner is the dog's side rails
            and its emergency stop. If you need to leave, stop the belt first.</li>
            <li><strong>The dog must always be able to step off.</strong> Keep the space to the
            sides and rear of the treadmill clear, and never box the dog in with furniture or a
            person standing behind it. A dog that wants to leave the belt must be able to do so
            instantly and without obstruction.</li>
            <li><strong>Remember the safety key is for a human.</strong> Insert it so the machine
            runs, then stand beside the treadmill with your hand near the key or the stop button
            for the whole session. Pull it the moment anything goes wrong. Never clip it to the
            dog.</li>
            <li><strong>Start at the lowest speed, and stay low.</strong> Begin at the slowest
            setting the machine offers and raise it in the smallest increments, only once the dog
            is relaxed and walking naturally. A human treadmill is for a walking dog; running is a
            job for a purpose-built machine.</li>
            <li><strong>Supervise from the side, not the front.</strong> Standing in front of the
            machine draws the dog forward into the motor hood. Standing beside it lets you see the
            paws, keep the dog centred, and reach the controls.</li>
            <li><strong>Keep sessions short.</strong> A few minutes at first, building slowly, and
            even for an experienced dog nothing like the length of a proper walk. Stop before the
            dog is tired, not after.</li>
            <li><strong>Stop at any sign of stress.</strong> Panting beyond what the effort
            explains, drooling, a tucked tail, flattened ears, whale eye, repeated attempts to
            step off, scrabbling, freezing, or any change in gait. Stop the belt, let the dog off,
            and end the session. If it happens again, stop using the treadmill with that dog.</li>
            <li><strong>Watch paws and heat.</strong> Check pads before and after for wear, heat or
            redness; a belt is an abrasive surface at speed. Keep the room cool and water
            available, and remember that dogs cannot cool themselves the way people do. A dog
            that is panting hard on a treadmill is being overworked.</li>
            <li><strong>Remove the key and unplug when finished.</strong> A dog cannot start a
            treadmill, but the household might, and a dog that has learned the belt is a place to
            go can be on it when someone does. The general rules in our
            <a href="/treadmill-safety-tips/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmill safety tips</a>
            apply to households with dogs as much as to those with children.</li>
          </ol>
          <p>
            A final rule that is less about the machine and more about judgement: if you find
            yourself working around any of the above — a quick tether just while you fetch
            something, a slightly faster speed because the dog seems fine — that is the moment to
            stop. The accidents owners describe almost always begin with a rule that was bent once.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="which-dogs-not" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">5. Which Dogs This Is Plainly Wrong For</h2>
<p>
            Some dogs should not be exercised on a moving belt at all, and the equipment
            compromises of a human treadmill make the margin worse. This is not an exhaustive
            list, and it is not a substitute for a vet's opinion on your own dog; it is the set of
            cases where the answer is already clear before the vet is asked.
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Puppies and adolescent dogs.</strong> Growing joints and growth plates are
            widely understood to be vulnerable to repetitive, forced exercise, and a motorised belt
            is exactly that: a fixed pace the dog cannot vary, on a hard surface, for a set time.
            Until a vet confirms the dog is physically mature, a treadmill is the wrong tool.</li>
            <li><strong>Senior dogs.</strong> Older dogs tire faster, hide pain better, and have
            less margin for a stumble or a twisted paw at the belt edge. Gentle outdoor walks on
            their own terms are almost always better.</li>
            <li><strong>Brachycephalic, or flat-faced, breeds.</strong> Dogs with shortened
            muzzles have compromised airways and struggle to cool themselves. Sustained exercise
            at a pace they cannot adjust, in an indoor room, is a poor combination for them, and
            overheating can escalate quickly.</li>
            <li><strong>Dogs with heart, joint or breathing conditions.</strong> Hip or elbow
            dysplasia, arthritis, cruciate injuries, heart murmurs, laryngeal problems — any of
            these is a reason not to proceed without specific veterinary guidance, and often a
            reason not to proceed at all.</li>
            <li><strong>Overweight dogs, without a vet-led plan.</strong> It is tempting to use a
            treadmill to slim down a heavy dog, but extra weight on joints and the heart is exactly
            when forced pace is riskiest. If a vet recommends treadmill exercise as part of a
            weight plan, follow their programme; do not improvise one.</li>
            <li><strong>Nervous, reactive or sound-sensitive dogs.</strong> A dog that startles at
            the vacuum cleaner will not enjoy a treadmill motor, and a frightened dog on a moving
            belt is the scenario the rules above are trying to prevent.</li>
            <li><strong>Very small dogs, on a human treadmill specifically.</strong> The minimum
            speed is often too fast, the belt edges and rear gap are large relative to the dog,
            and a small dog's stride makes the mismatch obvious. If a small dog is to use a
            treadmill at all, it should be one built for its size.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            For every other dog, the question is still "should this dog use this machine", and the
            vet is the person to ask. Dogs cannot tell you their knee hurts until it hurts quite a
            lot.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="training-a-dog" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">6. Introducing a Dog to a Moving Belt, Gradually</h2>
<p>
            A dog that is put on a running belt with no preparation learns one thing: the machine
            is frightening. The dogs that walk calmly on treadmills were introduced over days or
            weeks, in small steps, with the belt stationary for most of them. The outline below is
            how owners commonly describe it; a qualified trainer can tailor it to a particular
            dog, and a dog that does not progress happily through any step should not be pushed
            through it.
          </p>
          <ol>
            <li><strong>The machine off, for several sessions.</strong> Let the dog approach,
            sniff, and step on and off the stationary belt in its own time, with praise and a
            treat for calm behaviour on the deck. The treadmill should become a boring piece of
            furniture before it becomes a moving one.</li>
            <li><strong>The machine on, dog off.</strong> Run the belt at its slowest speed with
            the dog in the room but not on it, so the sound and movement become familiar. Keep
            these short and end them before the dog is bothered.</li>
            <li><strong>Standing on a stationary belt, then a starting one.</strong> With the dog
            standing comfortably on the stopped belt, and you beside it, start the belt at its
            lowest speed for a few seconds, praise, and stop. Build the duration over sessions,
            never the speed.</li>
            <li><strong>Walking at the lowest speed.</strong> Once the dog walks naturally at the
            minimum, hold there for as many sessions as it takes to look relaxed — loose body,
            normal tail carriage, easy gait. Many owners never need to go faster than a slow walk,
            and for a human treadmill that is the right ceiling.</li>
            <li><strong>Small increments, if any.</strong> If the dog is clearly comfortable and
            the machine allows a sensible next step, raise the speed by the smallest increment and
            repeat the process. A gentle trot is the most a human treadmill should be asked to
            provide; anything that looks like running means the deck is too short for the job.</li>
          </ol>
          <p>
            Throughout, you are beside the machine, the dog is untethered, and treats and praise
            come from the side rather than from the front. Luring a dog forward with food held
            ahead of it is a common mistake, because it draws the dog into the motor hood and
            teaches it to walk at the very front of the belt. Reward the dog for walking in the
            middle.
          </p>
          <p>
            If at any stage the dog shows the stress signs listed in the rules — or simply refuses
            — the honest conclusion is that this dog and this machine are not a match, and no
            amount of patience changes the equipment compromises. That is a perfectly good outcome;
            it means more walks.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="what-it-is-for" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">7. What a Treadmill Is, and Is Not, For a Dog</h2>
<p>
            Even done perfectly, a treadmill session gives a dog one thing: physical movement at a
            fixed pace in a fixed place. That is a real benefit in a narrow set of circumstances,
            and it is a very small part of what a walk provides.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>What it can do.</strong> Supplement exercise for a healthy, high-energy adult
            dog during weather that makes walks unsafe — ice, extreme heat — or during a period
            when the owner cannot walk as far as the dog needs. It can be useful under veterinary
            direction for controlled, measured exercise in a rehabilitation plan, where the fixed
            pace is the point. And some working and sporting dogs are conditioned on purpose-built
            machines as part of a structured programme run by people who do this professionally.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>What it cannot do.</strong> A walk is mostly not about the walking. It is
            sniffing — the main way a dog reads the world and the thing that tires a dog's brain
            far more than its legs. It is choosing a pace, stopping, changing direction, meeting
            other dogs and people, toileting, and spending unstructured time with the owner. A
            treadmill offers none of that, and a dog exercised only on one is physically tired and
            mentally underfed. Owners sometimes notice that a treadmill-walked dog is still
            restless, and that is why.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>So the honest framing is:</strong> a treadmill supplements walks; it does not
            replace them. If the reason for considering one is that the dog is not getting enough
            walks, the better equipment purchase is usually a good coat and a head torch, or the
            services of a dog walker. If the reason is a specific, temporary constraint, or a
            vet-led plan, a treadmill can earn its place — a purpose-built one more comfortably
            than a human one.
          </p>
          <p>
            A household that is already weighing who else will use the machine — children, older
            relatives, a partner who walks rather than runs — will find the same trade-offs
            discussed from the human side in our
            <a href="/family-treadmill-guide/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">family treadmill guide</a>.
            A dog is one more user with its own requirements, and in the case of a human treadmill,
            the one whose requirements the machine meets least well.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="what-it-does-to-the-machine" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">8. What a Dog Does to the Treadmill</h2>
<p>
            This is an equipment site, so it is worth being plain about the other direction: a dog
            is hard on a treadmill in ways a human user is not, and the consequences land on the
            belt, the deck and the motor.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Hair in the motor compartment.</strong> A treadmill motor is cooled by a fan
            drawing air through the hood, and a dog sheds continuously. Fur is drawn into the
            compartment, wraps around the fan and the drive belt, and settles on the control board
            and the motor. The consequences are an overheating motor, a drive belt that slips, and
            in the worst cases a burning smell as hair bakes on hot components. If a dog uses the
            machine at all, the motor compartment needs vacuuming far more often than the usual
            maintenance schedule suggests — monthly rather than a few times a year — and our guide
            to
            <a href="/how-to-vacuum-treadmill-motor-compartment/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">vacuuming the motor compartment</a>
            covers how, starting with unplugging the machine.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Hair, grit and claws under the belt.</strong> Fur and whatever is on the paws
            works its way between the belt and the deck, where it abrades the deck's low-friction
            surface and the underside of the belt. Claws score the belt's top surface over time.
            Both accelerate belt and deck wear, raise the motor's load, and shorten the interval
            before the belt needs lubricating or replacing. Lubricate more often than the manual
            suggests, check under the belt for debris, and expect a shorter belt life.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Drool, dander and the odd accident.</strong> Saliva and sweat from paws on the
            belt, and the occasional urine accident from a stressed dog, need cleaning before they
            dry into the belt texture or run into the deck edges. Our guide to
            <a href="/how-to-clean-a-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">how to clean a treadmill</a>
            covers the belt, the rails and the console; for a dog-used machine, add a wipe of the
            belt surface after every session and a check of the side rails and rear roller for
            hair.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Concentrated wear.</strong> A dog tends to walk in exactly the same place on the
            belt every time, often further forward than a human would, and its four-footed gait
            loads the belt differently. Expect uneven wear and the belt to track off-centre more
            readily than it would with a human user, which means checking alignment more often.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>The warranty question.</strong> Few treadmill warranties mention dogs, and few
            manufacturers would expect to cover damage from one. Fur-choked motors and claw-scored
            belts are the kind of thing a warranty inspection notices.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="when-to-buy-dog-treadmill" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">9. When to Buy a Purpose-Built Dog Treadmill Instead</h2>
<p>
            Everything above points the same way: a human treadmill can work for an occasional,
            supervised, slow walk with a suitable dog, and a purpose-built dog treadmill is the
            right tool for anything beyond that. These are the signs that the dog version is the
            one to buy — or that a treadmill is not the answer at all.
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>The dog needs to trot or run.</strong> A working breed, a sporting dog, or
            any dog whose exercise need is genuinely a run rather than a walk needs a deck sized to
            its stride, and a human treadmill does not have one.</li>
            <li><strong>The dog is small.</strong> The minimum speed, the rear gap and the belt
            edges on a human treadmill are all out of proportion for a small dog. Small-breed dog
            treadmills are built to the right scale.</li>
            <li><strong>Sessions will be regular.</strong> Daily use justifies a machine designed
            for the job, with side rails, a quiet drive and a lower deck, and spares the human
            treadmill the fur, claws and wear.</li>
            <li><strong>A vet has prescribed treadmill exercise.</strong> Rehabilitation programmes
            are built around controlled pace and duration, and a dog treadmill — or the clinic's
            own equipment — is what the programme assumes.</li>
            <li><strong>The dog would benefit from a self-paced belt.</strong> Manual and slatted
            dog treadmills move only as fast as the dog does, which removes the central hazard of a
            motorised belt for a dog that cannot operate a stop button.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            We do not review dog treadmills and will not recommend one here. What we can say from
            the equipment side is what to look for in any of them: a deck long enough for the
            dog's full stride at the fastest pace you intend to use, side rails or panels, a
            minimum speed that is a genuine slow walk for that dog, a quiet drive, a clear front
            with nothing at nose height, and a way to stop the belt instantly from beside the
            machine. Sellers that give the deck length and the speed range plainly are easier to
            compare; sellers that lead with a dog looking happy are not.
          </p>
          <p>
            And the honest alternative to both: more walks, a dog walker, a secure field to hire,
            or games that tire the dog's head. For most dogs most of the time, that is what the
            treadmill money is better spent on.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="checklist" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">10. A Pre-Session Checklist for a Human Treadmill</h2>
<p>
            For owners who have had the vet conversation, have a suitable adult dog, and intend to
            use a human treadmill for slow, supervised, supplementary walks, this is the checklist
            to run before every session. It is short on purpose.
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li>The vet has agreed this dog can do this, and nothing about the dog has changed
            since.</li>
            <li>The treadmill is on a level floor, with clear space to both sides and at least a
            couple of metres behind the belt.</li>
            <li>The motor compartment was vacuumed recently, the belt is clean and tracking
            centrally, and the machine starts and stops normally with the safety key.</li>
            <li>The dog is untethered. Collar and harness are fine; nothing attached to anything.</li>
            <li>The room is cool, water is to hand, and the dog's pads looked normal before
            starting.</li>
            <li>The speed is set to the minimum, and you have no intention of raising it beyond a
            walk.</li>
            <li>You are standing beside the machine with a hand near the stop button or key, the
            phone is elsewhere, and nobody is going to need you for the next few minutes.</li>
            <li>You know what stress looks like in this dog, and you will stop at the first sign of
            it.</li>
            <li>The session is planned to be short, and you will end it before the dog is tired.</li>
            <li>Afterwards: key out, machine unplugged, belt wiped, pads checked, and a proper walk
            still on the day's list.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            If any line on that list cannot be ticked, the session does not happen. That is the
            whole point of a checklist, and with a dog on a machine that was never designed for
            one, it is also the whole safety system.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>


      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">11. Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can dogs use a human treadmill?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Some healthy adult dogs can walk on one slowly, with an owner standing beside the machine throughout, after a vet has agreed the dog is a suitable candidate. The belt is short for a running dog, the rear roller gap and motor hood are hazards, and there are no side rails, so it is a compromise. Never tether the dog, never leave it, and stop at any sign of stress.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is a treadmill safe for a dog?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Only with strict supervision and the right dog. A motorised belt keeps moving when the dog stops, the dog cannot use the safety key, and a startled dog can step off sideways or drift into the motor hood. Purpose-built dog treadmills reduce these risks with side rails, longer decks and slower minimum speeds. A vet should be consulted before any dog is exercised on any treadmill.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What kind of dog should not use a treadmill?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Puppies and adolescent dogs with growing joints, senior dogs, flat-faced breeds that struggle to cool themselves, dogs with heart, joint or breathing conditions, overweight dogs without a vet-led plan, and nervous or sound-sensitive dogs. Very small dogs are also a poor match for a human treadmill specifically, because the minimum speed and belt gaps are out of proportion for them.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How do you get a dog used to a treadmill?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Gradually, over days or weeks, with the belt stationary for most of the steps: let the dog sniff and step on the switched-off machine, run it with the dog off, then start it at the lowest speed for a few seconds with the dog standing on it and you beside it. Build duration before speed, reward the dog for walking in the middle, and stop if it shows stress.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Does a dog on a treadmill damage the machine?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                It wears it faster. Shed fur is drawn into the motor compartment, where it wraps the fan and drive belt and can cause overheating; hair and grit under the belt abrade the deck; claws score the belt surface; and drool and accidents need cleaning before they dry. Vacuum the motor compartment monthly, lubricate more often, and expect a shorter belt life and possible warranty questions.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can a treadmill replace walking my dog?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                No. A treadmill provides movement at a fixed pace in a fixed place. A walk provides sniffing, choice, changes of direction, meeting other dogs and people, toileting and time with you, which tire a dog mentally as well as physically. A treadmill can supplement walks in bad weather or under a vet-led plan, but a dog exercised only on one is usually still restless.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div class="mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">Common Mistakes With a Dog on a Treadmill</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">Nearly every account of a treadmill injuring a dog involves one of these four, and the first two are the serious ones.</p>
          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Tethering the dog to the machine</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">A leash looped over the console or clipped to the handrail turns a stumble into a drag and a caught lead into a roller injury. The dog must be free to step off at any moment, with nothing attached to the treadmill, the wall or a person. If the dog will not stay on the belt without a tether, the dog has told you its answer.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Stepping away for a moment</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">The owner is the dog's side rails and its only emergency stop, because a dog cannot pull a safety key. Answering the door, fetching a treat or glancing at a phone is exactly long enough for a dog to drift into the motor hood or trail a paw towards the rear roller. If you must move, stop the belt first, every time.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Luring the dog forward with treats</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">Holding food in front of a dog on a belt draws it to the very front of the deck, where the motor hood sits at nose height, and teaches it to walk there. Reward from beside the machine, for walking in the middle of the belt, and keep the space ahead of the dog empty so there is nothing to push towards.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Treating the console speed as a target</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">A human treadmill is for a walking dog, and the right speed is the one at which the dog looks relaxed, not the one that seems like a workout. Raising the speed because the dog "seems fine" is how a gentle walk becomes a trot the deck is too short for. Stay at the minimum until there is a good reason not to, and never let it become a run.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

