        <!-- Closing CTA -->
        <div class="mt-16 bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-8">
          <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-white mb-4">The Bottom Line</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            <strong class="text-white">A human treadmill is a compromise for a dog, and the wrong
            tool for some dogs altogether.</strong> Some healthy adult dogs can walk on one slowly
            with an owner beside the machine, after a vet has agreed, under rules that are not
            negotiable: never tethered, never unattended, always able to step off, always at the
            lowest speed, always stopped at the first sign of stress.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            For a dog that needs to trot or run, a small dog, or regular use, a purpose-built dog
            treadmill is the right answer, and for most dogs more walks are the better one. If
            you do share your machine, expect fur in the motor and faster wear — our guide to
            <a href="/how-to-vacuum-treadmill-motor-compartment/" class="text-[#5AA9FF] font-bold no-underline">vacuuming the motor compartment</a>
            is about to become a monthly habit.
          </p>
